\ core::cmp -- the typed comparisons over a nominal `Ord` trait.
\
\ `Ord` is an ordinary library trait: `cmp ( 'T 'T -- Ordering )`, satisfied
\ nominally by one `impl:` block per numeric width. The six surface
\ comparisons (`eq`/`lt`/`gt`/`lte`/`gte`/`ne`) are derived from `cmp` and its
\ three-variant result, so the compiler itself needs no reserved notion of
\ "ordered" at all -- a user type opts in with its own `impl: Ord`, exactly as
\ any other trait.
\
\ `cmp` is an `inline` trait member (P7.S3s-follow): every `impl: Ord` body
\ is spliced at its call sites instead of costing a call frame, so the six
\ surface comparisons (`eq`/`lt`/`gt`/`lte`/`gte`/`ne`) get `cmp`'s body
\ spliced into them. The six comparisons are `inline` in turn, so a
\ comparison and its `Ordering?` eliminator fold into the caller and a
\ library comparison costs no call frame at all.
import: intrinsics i | ueq ult ugt ulte ugte une branch drop swap | ;
import: self::bool b | Bool False True | ;

export: Ord Ordering Less Equal Greater eq lt gt lte gte ne ;

\ Declaring these three variant names here effectively reserves them for
\ every program: a variant constructor's env key is the bare surface name
\ with no module in it, so a user enum with a variant named `Less`, `Equal`,
\ or `Greater` captures the constructor `lib/cmp.sth`'s own `impl: Ord`
\ bodies use, and the build fails inside `cmp`. The module-blind key is
\ pre-existing (two *user* modules sharing one variant name collide the same
\ way, and did before `Ordering` existed); what P7.S3s changes is that one of
\ the colliding parties is now a module every program reaches through the
\ prelude. Fixing it means keying variant constructors by module, which is
\ not this slice.
type: Ordering
| Less
| Equal
| Greater
;

trait: Ord['T]
  : cmp inline ( 'T 'T -- Ordering ) ;
;

\ The eight fixed-width integers (`INT_TYPES`, `src/ast.rs`) and
\ `usize`/`isize`: `ult`/`ugt` derive signed/unsigned/float behaviour from
\ the operand type at lowering, so one two-comparison body covers every
\ width -- `a < b` is `Less`, `a > b` is `Greater`, otherwise `Equal`. `branch`
\ reads the raw 32-bit flag `ult`/`ugt` themselves produce, the same
\ primitive `core::bool`'s `if` is built over, so no comparison here goes
\ through the `Bool` it has not yet built.
impl: Ord for i8
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for i16
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for i32
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for i64
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u8
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u16
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u32
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for u64
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for usize
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for isize
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Equal ] branch ] branch ;
;

\ Both floats. A NaN operand is the one case `Ordering`'s three variants
\ cannot represent: IEEE-754 makes every ordered comparison false, including
\ `x = x`, so a partial order wants a fourth "unordered" answer this enum has
\ no arm for. A real equality check (`ueq`) comes first so a genuinely equal
\ pair still answers `Equal`; only a NaN pair (neither `ueq`, `ult`, nor
\ `ugt` holds) falls to the `Greater` default. All six surface comparisons
\ stay IEEE-correct for NaN over that default: `eq`/`ne`/`lt`/`lte` read
\ `cmp(a,b)` while `gt`/`gte` read `cmp(b,a)` (see the note above `gt`), so a
\ NaN pair answers `False` for all five ordered relations and `True` for
\ `ne`. `eq` in particular stays load-bearing (D4, phase0: NaN is detected
\ via `x = x`).
\
\ The residual is `cmp` itself. It is not in `export:`, but `Ord` is, so any
\ user writing their own `'T: Ord` word dispatches `cmp` directly -- and
\ over floats it is not antisymmetric, since `cmp(a,b)` and `cmp(b,a)` are
\ both `Greater` when either operand is NaN. A `PartialOrd`/`Ordering?`-shaped
\ split giving the unordered case its own answer is the follow-on
\ (P7.S3s R5), not this slice.
impl: Ord for f32
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ueq [ Equal ] [ lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Greater ] branch ] branch ] branch ;
;

impl: Ord for f64
  : cmp
    | lhs rhs |
    lhs rhs ueq [ Equal ] [ lhs rhs ult [ Less ] [ lhs rhs ugt [ Greater ] [ Greater ] branch ] branch ] branch ;
;

: eq inline ['T: Ord] ( 'T 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop False ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop True ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: lt inline ['T: Ord] ( 'T 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop False ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

\ `gt`/`gte` compare with the operands swapped (`a > b` iff `b < a`, `a >= b`
\ iff `b <= a`, true for every value including a NaN pair under IEEE-754,
\ where every ordered relation is `False`) rather than reading `cmp(a,b)`'s
\ `Greater`/`Greater-or-Equal` arms directly. This is what keeps `gt`/`gte`
\ IEEE-correct for a NaN operand: `lib/cmp.sth`'s float `impl: Ord` answers
\ `Greater` for a NaN pair (review fix, so `eq`/`ne`/`lt`/`lte` stay correct
\ for NaN, preserving D4), which would otherwise make `gt`/`gte` answer
\ `True` for NaN if read directly off that same `Greater` arm.
: gt inline ['T: Ord] ( 'T 'T -- Bool )
  swap cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop False ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: lte inline ['T: Ord] ( 'T 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop True ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: gte inline ['T: Ord] ( 'T 'T -- Bool )
  swap cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop True ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop False ]
  Ordering? ;

: ne inline ['T: Ord] ( 'T 'T -- Bool )
  cmp
  ~[ ( Less )    drop True ]
  ~[ ( Equal )   drop False ]
  ~[ ( Greater ) drop True ]
  Ordering? ;
